syntax: parse expansions bash, sh and mksh reject only when run as text

Bash, POSIX shells such as dash, and mksh only check what follows ${ when
they expand it: bash -n, dash -n and mksh -n accept every one of

	echo ${.sh.file} ${(%):-%x} ${@name@} ${$x} ${} ${:-word}

and the shell fails with "bad substitution" only if the expansion runs.
Scripts rely on this to run another shell's expansion behind a version
check, as in

	if [ -n "${ZSH_VERSION:-}" ]; then
	  name=${(%):-%x}
	elif [ -n "${KSH_VERSION:-}" ]; then
	  name=${.sh.file}
	fi

and templates hold placeholders like ${@prefix@} that are filled in later.
The parser rejected such scripts, and the error took the rest of the
statement with it, so tools lost the whole if clause.

With LangBash, LangBats, LangPOSIX and LangMirBSDKorn, a ${ that cannot
start a parameter, or a special parameter followed by a word, is now parsed
as a literal word part holding the expansion's text, up to the } that
closes it outside of quotes and nested braces. Printing such a file gives
the input back unchanged. Expansions that start like a valid parameter,
such as ${a b} or ${1a}, still fail to parse, as do the same inputs with
LangZsh and LangKsh93, whose shells check more of them when parsing.

Reproducer, before this change: `x=${.sh.file}` with LangBash failed with
"invalid parameter name".

